* Roadmap

- This lesson starts with **logic-based AI under uncertainty**: why rules with
  exceptions cannot describe a partially observable, non-deterministic world, and
  why probability and utility are needed

- It then covers **probabilistic reasoning**: the full joint distribution, and how
  independence and conditional independence make it compact

- It ends with **Bayesian networks**: directed graphs plus conditional
  probability tables, illustrated with the Garden World and burglar alarm examples

# Logic-Based AI Under Uncertainty

## Limits of Logic-Based Systems

* Logic Rules Under Uncertainty

- @Definition@: **Logic-based AI systems**:
  - Are based on propositional logic
  - Represent actions using rules
    - E.g., _"If preconditions P hold, then action A causes effect E"_

- @Example@:
  - _"If I turn the car key, the engine starts"_
  - But the battery might be dead, there's no fuel, the starter is broken, ...

- @Problem@: Real-world agents face _uncertainty_ from:
  1. _Partial observability_
     - Agents can't see the full state of the world
  2. _Non-determinism_
     - Actions don't always have deterministic or predictable outcomes
  3. _Adversarial conditions_
     - Other agents may interfere

* Belief States and Contingent Plans

- @Naive Solution@: A possible solution to logic-based AI under uncertainty is to
  use:
  1. @Belief state@, i.e., set of all possible current world states
     - _Cons_: difficult to enumerate and track
  2. @Causal and exhaustive augmentation@ for rules
     - _Cons_: must consider all possible preconditions for acting, even unlikely
       ones
  3. @Contingent plans@ to handle every possible sensor report and belief
     - _Cons_:
       - Plans become large and complex
       - No guaranteed plan may exist, yet action is required

* Starting a Car

- @Goal@: build a system to start a car by turning the car key

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Obvious preconditions@
  - Car has fuel
  - Car battery is charged
  - Car key is the right one
  - ...

- @Less obvious preconditions@
  - Electrical system is intact
  - Fuel pump is working
  - Fuel lines are not clogged
  - Engine oil level is sufficient
  - ...

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz[width=75%]
digraph BayesianFlow {
    rankdir=LR;
    compound=true;
    splines=true;
    nodesep=0.1;
    ranksep=0.5;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4, fillcolor="#FFD1A6", height=0.3];

    Goal [label="Engine starts", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    subgraph cluster_obvious {
        label="Obvious"; labeljust=l; style="rounded,dashed"; color="#888888";
        fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=12;
        O1 [label="Fuel"];
        O2 [label="Battery charged"];
        O3 [label="Right key"];
    }

    subgraph cluster_hidden {
        label="Less obvious"; labeljust=l; style="rounded,dashed"; color="#888888";
        fontname="Helvetica"; fontsize=12;
        H1 [label="Electrical system"];
        H2 [label="Fuel pump"];
        H3 [label="Fuel lines clear"];
        H4 [label="Oil level"];
    }

    O2 -> Goal [ltail=cluster_obvious];
    H2 -> Goal [ltail=cluster_hidden];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Problem@: Every precondition requires _something else_ to achieve the goal

## The Qualification Problem

* Causal and Exhaustive Rules

- @Definition@: **Causal and exhaustive augmentation** of logic rules
  - To use propositional logic under uncertainty, need to augment left side of
    rules $X \implies Y$ to make it:
    1. _Causal_: identify true causal-effect relationships
    2. _Exhaustive_: identify all possible conditions $X$ leading to outcome $Y$

- @Limitations@:
  - Every action can have more hidden conditions needed to succeed
    - E.g., start the engine $\implies$ turn car key $\implies$ starter motor is
      working $\implies$ battery is charged $\implies$ ...
  - Preconditions can change depending on situation
    - E.g., ... $\implies$ battery is charged $\implies$ it's not too cold
      $\implies$ ...

* Why Qualification Fails

- @Definition@: **Logical qualification problem** is the need to enumerate all
  preconditions necessary for an action to succeed
  `#cite("mccarthy1980circumscription")`{=typst}

- @Limitations@: It is not possible to solve the logical qualification problem
  for several reasons `#cite("russell2020aima")`{=typst}
  1. **Laziness**: Too much effort required to create all possible rules
  2. **Theoretical ignorance**: Science doesn't always have complete theory of
     domain
     - E.g., medical science doesn't know all the "rules"
  3. **Practical ignorance**: Even if you knew all rules, you might not have all
     necessary info
     - E.g., not all required medical tests can be performed for particular
       patient

- @Remark@: The **AI winter** (late 1980s to early 1990s) followed
  - Expert systems built on hand-written rules promised more than they delivered
  - Real world is complex and open-ended
  - Logical rules can't capture all necessary and sufficient conditions

* Garden World: Rules with Exceptions

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- @Example@: consider propositions
  - $Rain$ = _"it rains"_
  - $WetGrass$ = _"the grass is wet"_

- @Question@: Is it true that $Rain \implies WetGrass$?
  - "$Rain \implies WetGrass$" is not true in general
  - If it rains but there is a cover over grass, grass will not be wet
  - If it rains but temperature is high, wet grass might dry quickly

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L06.1.grass_sprinkler.png)
::::
:::

- @Question@: What about the converse $WetGrass \implies Rain$?
  - "$WetGrass \implies Rain$" is not true in general
    - Grass could be wet because of sprinkler system
    - Grass could be wet because of morning dew

- @Remark@: to make the rules hold, also consider propositions $Cover$
  (_"protective cover over the grass"_), $Evaporate$ (_"water evaporates
  quickly"_), $Sprinkler$ (_"sprinkler system is on"_), and $Dew$ (_"morning
  dew"_)

* Garden World: Causal and Exhaustive Rules

- @Goal@: identify all exceptions and dependencies to make $X \implies Y$ hold

  1. **Causal**: _"If it rains, the grass is not covered, and the water does not
     evaporate quickly, the grass is wet"_
     - $Rain \land \lnot Cover \land \lnot Evaporate \land \ldots \implies WetGrass$

  2. **Exhaustive**: _"Grass is wet if it rains or sprinkler is on or morning
     dew"_
     - $WetGrass \implies Rain \lor Sprinkler \lor Dew \lor \ldots$

```graphviz[width=80%]
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.7];

    // Nodes
    Rain       [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    WetGrass   [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    Cover      [label="Cover", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Evaporate  [label="Evaporate", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Sprinkler  [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    Dew        [label="Dew", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    // Force ranks
    { rank=same; Cover; Evaporate; }
    { rank=same; Sprinkler; Dew; }

    // Edges
    Rain -> WetGrass;
    Rain -> Cover;
    Rain -> Evaporate;
    Cover -> WetGrass [label="blocks", style=dashed];
    Evaporate -> WetGrass [label="blocks", style=dashed];
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
    Dew -> WetGrass;
}
```

## Probability as the Solution

* Probability and Utility

- @Remark@: propositional logic alone is not enough to act under uncertainty

- @Solution@: Acting under uncertainty requires combining:
  - **Probability**: handle uncertainty and partial knowledge
    - E.g., agent's internal estimate of outcome likelihoods
  - **Utility functions**: evaluate desirability of each outcome
    - E.g., combine goals like punctuality, comfort, legal compliance

- @Key idea@: **Rational choice** = plan that maximizes expected utility
  `#cite("vonneumann1944gametheory")`{=typst}
  - Evaluate plans based on performance on average, given known info
  - Still success is not guaranteed

- @Example@: when to leave for the airport (missed flight $= -100$, best in bold)

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Leave before** | **Pr(catch)** | **Utility if caught** | **Expected utility** |
| ---------------- | ------------- | --------------------- | -------------------- |
| 90 min           | 0.95          | 10                    | 4.50                 |
| **120 min**      | **0.98**      | **7**                 | **4.86**             |
| 24 h             | 0.9999        | -20                   | -20.01               |
\endgroup

* Probability as Belief

- @Problem@: there is **no uncertainty in the actual world**
  - E.g., the grass is wet, but either it rained or not

- @Key idea@: **knowledge is subjective**
  - Probabilities relate to knowledge state, not to the real world
  - Updating knowledge changes probability statements

- @Example@
  - Initially, observe _"wet grass"_
    - From past data: $\Pr(Rain | WetGrass) = 0.8$
    - Current belief: _"80% chance it rained if grass is wet"_ (a posterior after
      the wet-grass evidence, and the prior for the next update)
  - Further observe _"sprinkler was on"_
    - Wet grass could be due to sprinkler, not rain
    - Belief changes: $\Pr(Rain | WetGrass \land Sprinkler) = 0.4$ (Bayesian
      update)
  - Weather report (assumed reliable) says there was no rain
    - Evidence overrides prior:
      $\Pr(Rain | WetGrass \land Sprinkler \land WeatherReport) \approx 0$
  - Each statement was correct given the evidence available at that time
    - New evidence revises the belief, without making the earlier statements
      false

# Probabilistic Reasoning

// From AIMA 13, Probabilistic reasoning (p. 425)

## Representing Uncertainty

* Full Joint Probability Distribution

- @Definition@: Consider the **full joint probability distribution** of a set of
  random variables $X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n$ assigns probability to every possible
  world `#cite("russell2020aima")`{=typst}:
  $$
  \Pr(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \dots, X_n = x_n)
  $$
  where _possible world_ is any assignment of values to all variables

- @Fact@: Full joint probability can answer any probabilistic query about domain

- @Cons@
  - Size grows exponentially $k^n$ with number of variables $n$ and values $k$
  - Impractical for real-world problems with many variables
  - Manually specifying each entry is tedious or impossible

- @Remark@: In the real world, many variables are **not fully dependent** on all
  others
  - _Conditional and absolute independence_ of random variables:
    - Reduces number of variables needed in model
    - Makes compact and structured representations possible
      - E.g., factorized probabilistic models, Bayesian networks

* Independence of Random Variables

- @Definition@: Two random variables $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** iff:
  $$
  \Pr(X, Y) = \Pr(X) \cdot \Pr(Y)
  $$

- _Equivalently_: Knowing $Y$ tells nothing about $X$
  $$
  \Pr(X | Y) = \Pr(X)
  $$
  - E.g., events "coin flip" and "weather" are independent
    $$
    \Pr(Coin=Heads | Weather=Rainy) = \Pr(Coin=Heads)
    $$

- @Consequence@: Independence of random variables:
  - Reduces number of parameters needed to model system
    $$
    \Pr(X_1 | X_2, X_3) = \Pr(X_1)
    $$
  - Allows factorization of joint distribution
    $$
    \Pr(X_1, X_2, X_3) = \Pr(X_1) \cdot \Pr(X_2) \cdot \Pr(X_3)
    $$

## Conditional Independence

* Conditional Independence

- @Definition@: Two random variables $X$ and $Y$ are **conditionally
  independent** given a random variable $Z$ iff knowing $Z$ makes $X$ and $Y$
  independent `#cite("koller2009pgm")`{=typst}:
  $$
  X \perp Y | Z \iff \Pr(X, Y | Z) = \Pr(X | Z) \cdot \Pr(Y | Z)
  $$

- @Example@:
  - $X$ = _"it is raining today"_
  - $Y$ = _"a person is carrying an umbrella"_
  - $Z$ = _"the weather forecast"_
  - Without knowing $Z$: an umbrella $Y$ suggests rain $X$, so they are dependent
  - Given $Z$: once forecast is known, an umbrella $Y$ gives no info about rain
    $X$, so $X \perp Y | Z$

- @Pros@
  - Conditional independence more common than absolute independence
  - Factorize joint conditional distribution into product of individual ones

* Fire, Toast, and Alarm

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}
- Consider (two events can **become independent** once we know a third event):
  - $Fire$ = _"there is a fire in the house"_
  - $Toast$ = _"someone burned toast"_
  - $Alarm$ = _"the fire alarm rings"_
  - $Call$ = _"a friend calls to check on you"_

- **Dependencies**
  - $Alarm$ depends on $Fire$ or $Toast$
  - $Call$ depends on whether $Alarm$ rings

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Fire   [label="Fire",       fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Toast  [label="Toast",      fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Alarm  [label="Alarm",      fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    Call   [label="Call",       fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    { rank = same; Fire; Toast; }
    { rank = same; Call; }

    Fire  -> Alarm;
    Toast -> Alarm;
    Alarm -> Call;
}
```
::::
:::

- **Conditional independence**
  - $Call$ is conditionally independent of $Fire$ and $Toast$ given $Alarm$
  - Once alarm rang, the specific cause doesn't affect whether friend calls
    $$\Pr(Call | Alarm, Fire, Toast) = \Pr(Call | Alarm)$$

- @Interpretation@: $Alarm$ ringing "blocks" the path from $Fire$ and $Toast$ to
  $Call$

* Explaining Away

::: columns
:::: {.column width=72%}
- @Example@: $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are independent causes of $WetGrass$
  (ignoring $Weather$)

- @Question@: Is $\Pr(Rain | Sprinkler) = \Pr(Rain)$?
  - Yes: $Rain \perp Sprinkler$ when $WetGrass$ is not observed

- @Question@: Is $\Pr(Rain | Sprinkler, WetGrass) = \Pr(Rain | WetGrass)$?
  - No: given wet grass, sprinkler on explains the wetness, so rain is less
    likely
  - $Rain \not\perp Sprinkler | WetGrass$

::::
:::: {.column width=28%}

```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Rain       [label="Rain",       fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Sprinkler  [label="Sprinkler",  fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    WetGrass   [label="WetGrass",   fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Definition@: **Explaining away** (collider) occurs when
  `#cite("pearl1988probabilistic")`{=typst}:
  - Two variables (causes) influence third variable (effect)
  - Observing effect creates dependence between causes
  - Evidence for one cause explains effect and reduces need to believe in other

- @Interpretation@: Observing $WetGrass$ "opens" the blocked path between causes

# Bayesian Networks

## Definition and Intuition

* Bayesian Networks: Definition

- Aka:
  - "Bayes nets"
  - "Belief networks"
  - "Probabilistic networks"
  - "Graphical models" (broader class)
  - "Causal networks" (arrows have special meaning)

- @Definition@: A **Bayesian network** `#cite("pearl1988probabilistic")`{=typst}
  is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) with:
  1. _Nodes_ $X_i$ corresponding to random variables (discrete or continuous)
  2. _Edges_ $X \to Y$ connecting nodes and representing dependencies among
     variables
     - E.g., $X \in Parents(Y)$, $Y \in Children(X)$, ancestors, spouses, ...
  3. A _conditional probability table_ (CPT or CPD) per node $X_i$, quantifying
     the effect of parents on node:
     $$
     \Pr(X_i | Parents(X_i))
     $$
     - If node has no parents, it has prior probability

* Bayesian Networks vs Propositional Logic

- @Remark@: Bayesian networks analogous for uncertain knowledge to propositional
  logic for definite knowledge
  - Propositional logic = rigid rules (`True` or `False`)
  - Bayesian networks = flexible, degrees of belief
  - Replace $X \implies Y$ with $\Pr(Y | X)$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- @Example@: Garden world
  - $R$ = "It is raining", $W$ = "The grass is wet"
  - Propositional logic: If $R \implies W$ and $R$ is true, then $W$ must be true
  - Bayesian network:
    $\Pr(R = True) = 0.2$, $\Pr(W | R) = 0.9$, $\Pr(W | \lnot R) = 0.1$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Rain [label="Rain (R)", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R) = 0.2"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass (W)", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(W | R) = 0.9\nP(W | not R) = 0.1"];

    Rain -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

- @Example@: Medical diagnosis
  - Propositional logic: _"Patient has disease $D$" $\implies$ "Patient has
    symptom $S$"_
  - Bayesian network: _"Probability of $S$ given $D$ is high, but not certain"_

* Bayesian Networks and the Joint Distribution

- @Theorem@: **Graph topology** and **conditional probabilities** are sufficient
  to specify the full joint distribution, (often) very concisely
  $$
  \Pr(x_1, \dots, x_n) = \prod_{i=1}^{n} \Pr(x_i | parents(X_i))
  $$

- Topology of network (nodes and edges) specifies conditional independence
  relationships
  - E.g., $X \to Y$ means _"$X$ has direct influence on $Y$"_ (not necessarily
    _$X$ causes $Y$_)

- Nodes are directly influenced by parents and indirectly by ancestors

- **How to build Bayesian Network**
  - Domain experts decide what relationships exist among variables, determining
    topology
  - Conditional probabilities can be specified or estimated

## Conditional Probability Tables

* Conditional Probability Table

- @Definition@: _Conditional Probability Table_ (CPT) encodes probability of
  node $X_i$ given parents $Parents(X_i)$
  $$
  \Pr(X_i | Parents(X_i))
  $$

- Each row of CPT contains conditional probability of node under conditioning
  case (possible combination of parent values)

- **Note**
  - Natural for discrete variables, but can extend to continuous variables
  - A finite table of probabilities summarizes a potentially unbounded set of
    circumstances (the exceptions that logic rules must enumerate)

* Traffic Jam CPT

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- Consider events:
  - $T$ = "Traffic Jam", $S$ = "Snow", $R$ = "Rush Hour"
- CPT models $\Pr(T | S, R)$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz[width=70%]
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Snow [label="Snow", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    RushHour [label="RushHour", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    TrafficJam [label="TrafficJam", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(T | S, R)"];

    { rank = same; Snow; RushHour; }

    Snow -> TrafficJam;
    RushHour -> TrafficJam;
}
```
::::
:::

\begingroup \scriptsize
| T 		| P(T\|S,R) | P(T\|S,-R) | P(T\|-S,R) | P(T\|-S,-R) |
|-------|----------|-----------|-----------|------------|
| True  | 0.80     | 0.10      | 0.50      | 0.05       |
| False | 0.20     | 0.90      | 0.50      | 0.95       |
\endgroup

- @Remark@: each column of the CPT sums to 1
  - For each combination of parent values, the probabilities of $T$ true and
    false sum to 1
  - Probabilities across different parent combinations need not sum to 1

- @Interpretation@
  - Traffic highly likely when both snow and rush hour
  - Traffic unlikely when neither condition holds

## Garden World Network

* Garden World Network

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- Consider Garden World with 5 variables
  - $Rain$: directly influenced by $Weather$
  - $Sprinkler$: influenced by $Weather$ (e.g., less likely when raining)
  - $WetGrass$: affected by both $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$
  - $Weather$: general environmental conditions (e.g., sunny, cloudy)
  - $StockMarketUp$: whether stock market is up or down

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

* Garden World: Fork Dependence

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **dependent** (marginally)
  $$Rain \not\perp Sprinkler \iff Rain \leftrightarrow Sprinkler$$
  - If no $Rain$, then $Weather = sunny$ and $Sprinkler$ more likely (sprinklers
    on in sunny weather)
  - $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ related since they share common parent $Weather$

- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **conditionally independent** given $Weather$
  $$Rain \perp Sprinkler | Weather$$
  - Once $Weather$ fixed, knowing if it rained tells nothing about $Sprinkler$
  - Correlation "explained" by $Weather$ (confounder)

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

* Fork and Collider Together

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **dependent** given $WetGrass$ (with $Weather$ not
  observed)
  $$Rain \not\perp Sprinkler | WetGrass$$
  - Given wet grass, sprinkler off increases the chance of rain (and vice versa)

- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **dependent** given $WetGrass$ and $Weather$
  $$Rain \not\perp Sprinkler | WetGrass, Weather$$
  - Sunny: rain unlikely, sprinkler likely, so wet grass and sprinkler off
    $\implies$ must have $Rain$

- **Fork path** (common cause) is blocked by observing $Weather$, **collider
  path** (common effect) is opened by observing $WetGrass$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

* Garden World: Independent Node

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- $StockMarketUp$ is **(unconditionally) independent** of all other variables
  - It has no edges: absence of an edge encodes independence
  - Change in $StockMarketUp$ doesn't affect $Weather$

- @Question@: Is the independence assumption realistic?
  - In the past, there was a dependency of the stock market on $Weather$
    - Correlation between sunny weather in NYC and higher market index returns
    - Cold weather requires more heating oil
  - If the dependence matters, add the edge $Weather \to StockMarketUp$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

## Burglar Alarm Example

* Burglar Alarm: Network

- Famous example from Judea Pearl `#cite("pearl1988probabilistic")`{=typst}
- $Alarm$ system installed at home in LA
  - Detects $Burglary$, but also responds to minor $Earthquake$
- Two neighbors, $John$ and $Mary$ will $Call$ when they hear $Alarm$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- Structure of graph shows:
  - $Burglary$ and $Earthquake$ affect $Alarm$
  - $JohnCalls$ and $MaryCalls$ depend only on $Alarm$, not on $Burglary$ and
    $Earthquake$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Burglary [label="Burglary", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Earthquake [label="Earthquake", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Alarm [label="Alarm", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    JohnCalls [label="JohnCalls", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];
    MaryCalls [label="MaryCalls", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];

    { rank = same; Burglary; Earthquake; }
    { rank = same; JohnCalls; MaryCalls; }

    Burglary -> Alarm;
    Earthquake -> Alarm;
    Alarm -> JohnCalls;
    Alarm -> MaryCalls;
}
```
::::
:::

* Burglar Alarm: Probabilities

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- Probability of:
  - $Burglary$ is 0.001
  - $Earthquake$ is 0.002

- $Alarm$ system:
  - Fairly reliable at detecting $Burglary$
  - Responds to minor $Earthquake$ (false positives)

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Burglary** | **Earthquake** | **P(Alarm\| B,E)** |
| ------------ | -------------- | -------------------- |
| True         | True           | 0.95                 |
| True         | False          | 0.94                 |
| False        | True           | 0.29                 |
| False        | False          | 0.001                |
\endgroup

- $Burglary$ and $Earthquake$ are independent causes, so the probability of an
  alarm sums over their values:
  $$
  \Pr(Alarm) = \sum_{b, e} \Pr(Alarm | b, e) \Pr(b) \Pr(e) \approx 0.0025
  $$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Burglary [label="Burglary", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(B) = 0.001"];
    Earthquake [label="Earthquake", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(E) = 0.002"];
    Alarm [label="Alarm", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(A | B,E)"];
    JohnCalls    [label="JohnCalls",    fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(J | A)"];
    MaryCalls    [label="MaryCalls",    fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M | A)"];

    { rank = same; Burglary; Earthquake; }
    { rank = same; JohnCalls; MaryCalls; }

    Burglary   -> Alarm;
    Earthquake -> Alarm;
    Alarm      -> JohnCalls;
    Alarm      -> MaryCalls;
}
```
::::
:::

* Burglar Alarm: Neighbors

- Two neighbors, $John$ and $Mary$ will $Call$ when they hear $Alarm$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- **John**
  - Almost always $Call$s when hears alarm
  - Sometimes confuses telephone with $Alarm$ and $Call$s (false positives)

- **Mary**
  - Misses alarm 30% of cases (false negatives)

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
\vspace{-0.5cm}
\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(JohnCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.90            |
| False     | 0.05            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(MaryCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.70            |
| False     | 0.01            |
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Burglar Alarm: CPTs

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- Node without parents has unconditional (prior) probability

- Probabilities of a variable's values sum to 1 for each parent combination
  - Store only $\Pr(X = True | \ldots)$, the other value is redundant

- Boolean node with $k$ Boolean parents has $2^k$ rows

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
\vspace{-1cm}
\begingroup \scriptsize
| **P(Burglary)** |
| --------------- |
| .001            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(JohnCalls)** | **P(-JohnCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.90            | 0.10            |
| False     | 0.05            | 0.95            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Burglary** | **Earthquake** | **P(Alarm)** |
| ------------ | -------------- | ---------- |
| T            | T              | .95        |
| T            | F              | .94        |
| F            | T              | .29        |
| F            | F              | .001       |
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Summary

- @Key idea@: logic rules cannot enumerate every precondition of an action, so
  agents represent uncertainty with probabilities, and Bayesian networks make
  this tractable through conditional independence

- @Recap@
  - _Logic under uncertainty_: partial observability, non-determinism, and the
    qualification problem make exhaustive rules impossible
  - _Probability_: encodes degrees of belief that are revised as evidence
    arrives, and combines with utilities to define rational choice
  - _Independence_: independence and conditional independence tame the
    exponential size of the full joint distribution
  - _Bayesian networks_: a DAG plus one CPT per node specifies the joint
    distribution
  - _Fork and collider paths_: observing a common cause blocks dependence, while
    observing a common effect creates it (explaining away)

